 For Mother\n\nImage: Front CoverRuth Schwartz CowanBasic Books, Nov 1, 1983 - Social Science - 257 pages This book has a dual focus. As its title is meant to suggest, it is a history not just of housework but also of the tools with which that work is done: household technology. Human beings are tool-using animals; indeed, some anthropologists believe that, along with speech,, the ability to use and to refine our tools is precisely what sets us apart from other species of primates.\n\n### From inside the book\n\n[Input][Input][Input] [Input]\n\n### Contents\n\nChapter  | 5\n | \nChapter  | 16\n | \n\nChapter 3  | 40\n | \nCopyright  | \n | \n\n6 other sections not shown\n\n### Other editions - View all\n\n | More Work For Mother: The Ironies Of Household Technology From The Open ...\nRuth Schwartz Cowan\nNo preview available - 1985\n\n### Common terms and phrases\n\nadvertisements American appliances automatic flour mill baking became Beecher began Boston bread Burlend cash changes chap chapter Chicago chores Christine Frederick City classes cleaning clothing coal comfortable commercial laundries communities companies cooking cooperative corn decades Diary domestic refrigeration domestic service early electric Elizabeth example factories fairly families farm fireplace Frances Trollope fuel gas refrigerator grain Harriet Beecher Stowe history of housework Home Economics household technology housekeeping housewife housewives housework husband industrialization iron Journal kitchen labor laundering Lynd manufacturers Mary Hallock Foote meal meant meat Middletown mother needed nineteenth century Oliver Evans oven percent poor pots preparation purchased quickbreads rich rural Ruth Schwartz Cowan servants sewing Siegfried Giedion social standard of living Study tasks technological systems tion transportation twentieth United urban vacuum cleaner wages washing machine wheat woman women wood workers York young Zanesville\n\n### Bibliographic information\n\nTitle  | More Wor